{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>But indeed your blood.</b> To make clear that what He permitted of the body He permitted only with the soul of motion, but not the soul of man — and this is: ״ואך את דמכם לנפשותיכם אדרוש״ — 'but indeed your blood, for your souls, I will demand.' And this verse further teaches you that one who kills himself is liable, and the Holy One, blessed be He, will demand his blood from his own hand, for the word לנפשותיכם ('for your souls') is as though it said מנפשותיכם ('from your souls')." "<b>Alternatively.</b> It may be said that the lamed is superfluous: it says ואך את דמכם — that is, your souls — and this lamed is like the lamed of (1 Chronicles 3:2) ״השלישי לאבשלום בן מעכה״ — 'the third was Absalom the son of Maacah' — whose meaning is 'the third was Absalom.'" "<b>From every beast I will demand it.</b> That YHWH, blessed be He, will cause it to die. And there was an incident involving a rooster that killed a person, and they brought it to a court and stoned it. And 'from the hand of man' — if there are no witnesses in the matter, I Myself will demand his blood; and if there are witnesses in the matter, it is given over to the court. And this is it."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {1 "Rabbeinu Bahya compares the prefixed lamed in לנפשותיכם to the prefixed lamed in the Chronicler's genealogy formula, understood as explicative/equative."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:26.068750964Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}